<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#waqf">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Waqf</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ḥabs, habous, vakıf, awqāf</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A waqf, also called a ḥabs, is an irrevocable trust in Islamic law, usually an endowment of real estate or other assets whose revenues serve a specified purpose, such as a mosque, school, fountain or the founder's family. Once declared, the property cannot be sold, transferred or given away and is legally held to belong to God; the founder appoints a trustee to manage it. The practice rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a>, among them Umar's endowment of land at Khaybar on Muhammad's advice, but the formal system emerged from the 9th century: the oldest dated deed, of 876 CE, concerns a Qur'an now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> Waqf manages the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">al-Aqsa Mosque</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Samuel appointed Mohammad Amin al-Husseini Grand Mufti of Jerusalem in 1921, and in 1922 he became president of the Supreme Muslim Council, controlling the <b>Waqf</b> and the Islamic courts; the rivalry of his clan with the Nashashibis dominated Palestinian politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…guardians of proper Islamic government, restored Maliki Sunnism as the official religion after Almohadism, allied with the Maliki ulama of the cities and with the sharifian families, with whom they sometimes intermarried, and in Fez insisted on appointing the officials of religious institutions and managing the <b>waqf</b> endowments.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Religious law was administered by qadis following the Hanafi school, while charitable endowments (<b>waqfs</b>) financed mosques, madrasas, hospitals and caravanserais.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…built institutions that served religious and public life: the madrasa, the college of religious learning, which spread from the 11th century; the <b>waqf</b>, the charitable endowment that funded mosques, schools, hospitals, fountains and caravanserais; courts led by judges, the qadis; and Sufi lodges that offered hospitality and teaching.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>They built mosques, madrasas and tombs in Cairo and endowed them as <b>waqf</b>, partly to provide for their descendants, and they rose and fell with their factions.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
